#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/id #uuid "70291b7a-ae46-5e8a-b24b-3ea343eb84f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13195" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $S=\\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}: {-3} \\leq 2n+1 \\leq 7 \\}?$\n\n- $\\{ -2, 0, 4 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ 0, 3 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ -3, 1, 3 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $S.$ Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2n+1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4\\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2\\le n & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{ -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{ -2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\{ 0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $\\{ -3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/id #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13196" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{ a, b, c \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/8323a4292a4f06d66d456bfce1906cbc7e9933a3fac41dd5fb643e13d25f6a6e.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13197" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/id #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13198" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{ n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{ n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}?$\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$\n\n- $B\\nsubseteq A$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10,$ that is, $n\\geq 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5,$ that is, $n\\geq 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A.$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afcd0c4a-83d2-5752-ae51-e865413f5934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r},{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366679} {:answer/id #uuid "cec42cfe-6eae-589b-a99f-3ef70776533b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366680} {:answer/id #uuid "376440eb-7d5f-5251-a663-e5d5a4ce50cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r},{t},{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366681} {:answer/id #uuid "94674723-4ab3-52de-85dc-511572ad4dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{r},{t},{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366682} {:answer/id #uuid "03112bdb-2e89-56ab-beef-902da9572cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94674723-4ab3-52de-85dc-511572ad4dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{r},{t},{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366682} :answer-field/id #uuid "b48d7cc0-deda-54c4-a5a3-ddc79936f9e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17cce24b-1209-590f-8569-68896def4649" :question/math-academy-id "q-104924" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $S={r,t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $S,$ we list out every combination of elements within $S.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070456/assets/022399e6a7a5e1f79b58e856aa8e9e5e9f3561e8e4bcb5b80a95237d30d06002.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{r},{t},{r,t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc2ff121-820b-52c2-b693-d6ed16de88ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0},{1},{-1,0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300896} {:answer/id #uuid "24f5e149-b2c2-5e2d-96a1-353036ed393e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{0},{1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300897} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fea61a-e1a7-5363-bed2-b0a8632c866b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0},{-1},{1},{-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300898} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2d551e-b3f4-522e-9001-14569ed6604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300899} {:answer/id #uuid "3bee4e3f-eba6-5c0e-8b3e-45c07d7bbf4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{1},{1,2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24f5e149-b2c2-5e2d-96a1-353036ed393e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{0},{1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300897} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dff9a6a-b9cf-56f0-80c2-36ecdda2b744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfdf525d-068e-5e8a-a728-e4a1792f0ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104925" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A={0,1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/bb31e2bd632f39706b2785ce6cab764673997fe77b8a9417f7dc213cdff4b7a7.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set, since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{0},{1},{0,1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2e22a02-b744-5948-8be1-8718ed9ade7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366686} {:answer/id #uuid "58576262-6947-542b-ae10-b68d549cff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366687} {:answer/id #uuid "5f21bb08-5a57-58bd-954b-dd9682883434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366688} {:answer/id #uuid "172622cd-f422-5c25-9ce1-1c3c4507c66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366689} {:answer/id #uuid "36c89114-7b26-5ecd-825e-c7d06dd1d63b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58576262-6947-542b-ae10-b68d549cff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366687} :answer-field/id #uuid "03816318-2156-51fb-a9d6-f71971bcf414" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d87f299-b49e-50b2-9b8e-197c261ee00f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105065" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z∣x\\le 5}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣-7\\le x<3}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $-7\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false.  For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d44517ee-3d6f-507f-8374-71773c43e79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300931} {:answer/id #uuid "196cd7ad-5bf1-5c90-9fea-ae0a5e8795de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300932} {:answer/id #uuid "6805c159-44f8-5f1b-84c6-f9503dadd760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300933} {:answer/id #uuid "59339c0a-97aa-5ed7-a9d7-2497c86de036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300934} {:answer/id #uuid "0dabb692-b809-5f64-8d4b-add3d249ec5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1,3,5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59339c0a-97aa-5ed7-a9d7-2497c86de036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300934} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe02cb1-6b45-55e8-9609-6c2856bed3a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d9bbfa7-b5b1-52d4-bc25-0a500880f2e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-1189" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={1,2,3,4}?$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$\n\nNotice that the empty set ${}$ is a subset of any set.\n\nTherefore, ${}$ is a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4bea7f3-823b-5a2e-b495-47442814f85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366693} {:answer/id #uuid "d9920768-e99e-5c22-8b0c-0bfd7108317e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{+}⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366694} {:answer/id #uuid "0478eeb5-74b2-5ac3-977d-4d9b667a50dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{-}⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366695} {:answer/id #uuid "1db94549-6799-5eaa-927f-0f288543862b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366696} {:answer/id #uuid "1599f606-1325-5108-8444-b3d33f666c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆{Z}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1599f606-1325-5108-8444-b3d33f666c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆{Z}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366697} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebdd904d-b3f1-598e-b1b2-5fd708840195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "274e241e-a0c2-5612-a090-edf98e09aacd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1209" :question/problem "Which statement is **not** true?" :question/worked-solution "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $Z⊆{Z}^{+}$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every integer is a positive integer. For example, we have $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Z}^{+}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc24b2d-aefe-5550-8320-e73223adc725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051366} {:answer/id #uuid "b21fb707-a876-58e3-8917-4477dbbc31bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051367} {:answer/id #uuid "ece9a89e-b697-565f-9db7-d7d4ea7f957d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051368} {:answer/id #uuid "3d705157-90e1-541c-bdbc-dbf6b491c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051369} {:answer/id #uuid "429e9c42-0358-5a78-bf17-15026921143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "429e9c42-0358-5a78-bf17-15026921143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051370} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d45657-6892-5416-b2b9-12620528d63a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0906e8f1-346b-507f-9289-d9fb6006311a" :question/math-academy-id "q-1210" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5892d4db-ee17-5a9b-bb7d-4dabe3624e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={0,1,2,4,6,8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300945} {:answer/id #uuid "87bb18dc-9899-5965-962b-0ce6916f989c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={-1,0,1,2,4,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300946} {:answer/id #uuid "38af7345-898b-5fad-87ca-5b2b33366056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={1,3,5,7,9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300947} {:answer/id #uuid "18a1bd9e-7f78-57c3-b75d-8ce5a365964f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,6,12,14,32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300948} {:answer/id #uuid "17e08c17-c0a4-5c01-92e5-c8cea1cf8431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,4,6,7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a1bd9e-7f78-57c3-b75d-8ce5a365964f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,6,12,14,32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300948} :answer-field/id #uuid "1864bcf5-a777-5135-8aef-787b82f38f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2265169c-0d8b-562a-b72b-43f80dabce9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37265" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={2n∣n\\in N}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $A.$ The elements of the set \n$A$ are all the natural even numbers.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, a subset of $A$ is a set whose elements are also elements of the set $A.$\n\nIn our case, $B={2,6,12,14,32}$ is a subset of $A.$\n\nIn the rest of the options, the set $B$ has elements that are not in $A,$ meaning that it is not a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d6c89f0-2a1a-5531-b5d8-831e882897bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300959} {:answer/id #uuid "e6865ba6-79cb-5696-ab09-d0be9337b7cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300960} {:answer/id #uuid "639e0609-5927-50e2-922f-0b5aa58cc005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300961} {:answer/id #uuid "888a6d2c-da5e-5d94-b324-cd6d72385bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300962} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d2d22b-0b13-5678-876c-b0cb42f1ec3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d6c89f0-2a1a-5531-b5d8-831e882897bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300959} :answer-field/id #uuid "2783eb9e-a3b8-546f-9d63-ec7ede85ec61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c9ea526-3175-579e-bce7-db2dce376c08" :question/math-academy-id "q-506" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n\n- ${Q}^{+}$ (the set of positive rationals)\n- ${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)\n- $N$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{+}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every positive rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every negative integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd47303-153d-53c9-8585-16b4eedb092f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-\\pi },{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300980} {:answer/id #uuid "4ceeaee3-0de2-58b2-a573-99d13b8055a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300981} {:answer/id #uuid "15cf81d1-eb58-5d38-b739-c57b9a661a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300982} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3047d4-2fac-561d-98c5-8e63d74fe94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300983} {:answer/id #uuid "884a03de-7ffd-5a19-b4db-73c4a19e7aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d3047d4-2fac-561d-98c5-8e63d74fe94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300983} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ba15e2-1571-5139-a1fb-3c2b08335857" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00bdd032-b9a1-5a22-aa27-2be14a896901" :question/math-academy-id "q-80809" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $U={-\\pi ,\\pi }.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $U,$ we list out every combination of elements within $U.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/b0e483a2d10cba17fb2bd3bf0e78d7b4d2fc07b22dbb5c492564b998bbfce6fc.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }.\n$$"}]}
